{% trace-scenario title="Build or buy for LLM observability?" prompt="Your team is shipping a multi-agent system in three months. You need tracing, evaluation, cost visibility, and production confidence before launch. Langfuse offers open-source, self-hosted tracing with full data control. Uncoil offers integrated tracing, evaluation, and cost tracking as one managed product. Which decision reduces risk and gets you to a defensible launch?" outcome="The right choice depends on a single constraint: is your bottleneck infrastructure control and data residency (Langfuse), or is it evaluation velocity and cost transparency delivered without a platform-engineering project (Uncoil)? Get this wrong and you either violate a compliance requirement or spend six extra weeks building plumbing you didn't need to build." /%}

Every team evaluating LLM observability eventually asks some version of the same question: do we run this ourselves, or do we buy a platform that already does the hard parts? Langfuse and Uncoil sit on opposite sides of that question, and the difference is not cosmetic. It shapes your engineering calendar, your headcount allocation, and — six months in — your actual production confidence.

This is a long comparison because the honest answer is not "Uncoil is better" or "Langfuse is better." It is "these two products optimize for different constraints, and you need to know which constraint actually binds your team before you pick one." We'll walk through the architecture, three realistic production scenarios with real numbers, a feature-by-feature breakdown, worked total-cost-of-ownership math at two different scales, and the specific situations where Langfuse is genuinely the correct call — not a consolation prize.

## At a glance

| Dimension | Langfuse | Uncoil |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Model** | Open-source, self-hosted, plus a commercial cloud tier | SaaS-only, commercial |
| **Core strength** | Flexible, auditable, self-hosted LLM tracing; you own the data | Integrated tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, and sessions as one product |
| **Self-hosting** | Full support: Docker, Postgres, Redis, ingestion workers | Not available |
| **Evaluation framework** | Manual dashboard scoring plus API-based automation you build | Native test-case library, batch runner, LLM-as-judge, experiments |
| **Cost tracking** | Token counts at the span level; you supply pricing and math | Automatic per-call cost breakdown by model and provider |
| **Session tracking** | Trace metadata; you build session correlation | Native multi-turn sessions with context preservation |
| **Production readiness** | Strong tracing; evaluation and cost are DIY layers | Complete out of the box: tracing, evaluation, cost, sessions |
| **Setup time** | Self-hosted: 2-3 weeks. Cloud: about 1 week | About 15 minutes to first trace |
| **Team required for ops** | 0.5-1.5 FTE for self-hosted infrastructure | Roughly 0.1 FTE, mostly product-facing |
| **Steady-state annual cost (100k calls/month)** | Self-hosted: ~$129,600. Cloud: ~$51,000 | ~$30,600 |
| **Best for** | Data residency requirements, infrastructure control, specialized evaluation pipelines | Evaluation velocity, cost transparency, small-to-mid teams shipping fast |

### The operating model, in one paragraph

**Langfuse** is built around *tracing transparency and operational control*. You either run the entire stack inside your own infrastructure or use their managed cloud, and either way you compose evaluation, cost analysis, and experimentation from separate tools as your team needs them. **Uncoil** is built around *integrated evaluation and fast production iteration*: tracing, a native evaluation framework, automatic cost tracking, and session management ship together as one product, with nothing to self-host. Pick Langfuse when data residency, architectural control, or a highly specialized evaluation pipeline are hard requirements. Pick Uncoil when evaluation velocity, cost visibility, and time to first insight matter more than owning the infrastructure underneath your traces.

## Why this decision is bigger than a feature checklist

{% highlight %}The choice between Langfuse and Uncoil is not primarily about feature parity or sticker price. It is about whether your team's path to production confidence is bounded by infrastructure setup and glue code, or by a platform's built-in evaluation and cost-tracking capabilities.{% /highlight %}

Both products trace LLM calls well. Neither one is "bad" at the core job of capturing spans, tokens, and latency. The separation shows up one layer up, in what happens after the trace exists. Langfuse hands you a complete, auditable record of what happened and lets you decide what to build on top of it. Uncoil decides for you — evaluation, cost math, and session rollups are already there, wired into the same data you're already collecting.

Neither position is wrong. A team with two ML engineers and a mature Kubernetes practice can turn Langfuse's flexibility into a genuine advantage: custom scoring functions, proprietary evaluation models, a data warehouse pipeline that already exists. A ten-person startup trying to hit a launch date does not have that luxury, and every week spent building an evaluation runner is a week not spent improving the agent itself. The [complete guide to agent observability](/blog/ai-agent-observability-complete-guide) covers what a trace actually needs to capture regardless of platform — this article is about what happens once that data exists.

## Three realistic production scenarios

Numbers below assume a team of roughly twelve engineers, a customer-facing multi-agent product routing between Claude, GPT-4, and Mixtral, and about 100,000 LLM calls per month unless a scenario says otherwise. These are representative figures based on typical infrastructure and staffing costs, not a quote from either vendor — use them to reason about your own scale, not as guaranteed pricing.

### Scenario 1: Shipping a cost-aware routing agent in eight weeks

Your team is building a support agent that routes requests to different models by estimated complexity, then takes actions based on the response. Leadership wants three things simultaneously: a working product in eight weeks, visibility into cost per request by model, and an automated way to catch quality regressions before they reach customers.

**With Langfuse, self-hosted.** Weeks one through three go to infrastructure: a Postgres primary and read replica, Redis, and ingestion workers on Kubernetes, plus security hardening and monitoring. Budget roughly $950-1,200 a month in infrastructure and two engineers for most of those three weeks. Instrumentation itself is straightforward — Langfuse's SDKs are mature — but token counts arrive without prices attached, so cost math is something you build, not something you get.

```typescript
// Illustrative: span-level instrumentation with Langfuse
import { Langfuse } from "langfuse";

const langfuse = new Langfuse();

async function routeAndExecute(userMessage: string) {
  const trace = langfuse.trace({ name: "support-agent-run" });

  const routingSpan = trace.span({ name: "routing-decision" });
  const complexity = await analyzeComplexity(userMessage);
  const selectedModel = complexity > 0.7 ? "gpt-4" : "claude-3";
  routingSpan.end({ output: selectedModel });

  const llmSpan = trace.span({ name: "llm-call", input: { model: selectedModel } });
  const response = await callLLM(selectedModel, userMessage);
  // Token counts arrive on the response; cost still has to be calculated yourself
  llmSpan.end({ output: response, metadata: { tokens: response.usage } });
}
```

Weeks four through eight go to evaluation, and this is where the schedule gets tight. You need an evaluation runner that pulls traces from the Langfuse API, applies your quality checks (LLM-as-judge calls, regex matching, semantic similarity), stores results somewhere durable, and flags regressions. You also need to integrate provider pricing manually so "cost per request" means anything at all. Realistically, this team has traces and a rough cost report by week five or six, and a working quality gate by week seven or eight — right at the deadline, with no slack if anything slips.

**With Uncoil.** Setup is fifteen to thirty minutes: create an account, grab an API key, add the SDK.

```typescript
// Illustrative: equivalent instrumentation with Uncoil
import { Uncoil } from "@uncoil/sdk";

const uncoil = new Uncoil({ apiKey: process.env.UNCOIL_API_KEY });

async function routeAndExecute(userMessage: string) {
  const session = uncoil.session({ userId: user.id, sessionId: sessionId });

  const routingSpan = session.span({ name: "routing-decision" });
  const complexity = await analyzeComplexity(userMessage);
  const selectedModel = complexity > 0.7 ? "gpt-4" : "claude-3";
  routingSpan.end({ output: selectedModel });

  const llmSpan = session.span({ name: "llm-call", input: { model: selectedModel } });
  const response = await callLLM(selectedModel, userMessage);
  llmSpan.end({ output: response }); // Cost is calculated automatically
}
```

By day two, traces are flowing with automatic cost attribution per call. Test cases — "route to GPT-4 when complexity exceeds 0.7," "response must not contain profanity" — go into the dashboard directly, and batch evaluation runs against the traces you already have. Cost alerts ("notify us if average session cost exceeds $0.50") take minutes to configure, not a data-warehouse integration. By day three, the team has traces, cost visibility, and a running quality gate, and the remaining seven and a half weeks go to the actual product: tuning the routing logic against real cost and quality data instead of building the instrumentation to see it.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Production velocity" title="Integrated evaluation changes what the calendar looks like, not just what the dashboard shows." body="With Langfuse, both teams get excellent trace data — that part is not in question. What differs is how many weeks separate 'we can see what happened' from 'we can act on what happened.' By week eight, a Langfuse self-hosted team is often just finishing evaluation plumbing; an Uncoil team has been iterating on routing decisions against real cost and quality metrics since day three. That five-to-six-week gap compounds every sprint after launch, not just before it." tone="yellow" /%}

### Scenario 2: A regulated team where data cannot leave the VPC

Your company operates in financial services or healthcare. Compliance requires that production data — including prompts, model outputs, and any PII embedded in them — never leaves your network boundary. This is not a preference; it is a hard audit requirement with a named compliance officer attached to it.

**With Langfuse, self-hosted.** This is the scenario self-hosting exists for. The entire platform — ingestion, Postgres, Redis — runs inside your VPC, behind your firewalls, under your existing access controls. Because Langfuse is open-source, your security team can read the code that touches sensitive data, not just trust a vendor's SOC 2 report. Evaluation and cost analysis get wired into your existing data warehouse or ML platform rather than a third party's servers. The operational cost is real — plan on 0.5 to 1.5 FTE and $1,100-1,300 a month in infrastructure at this scale — but it is the price of a genuine yes to "does any of this leave our network," not a workaround.

**With Uncoil.** There isn't a version of this scenario where Uncoil fits. It is SaaS-only; trace data lives in Uncoil's infrastructure, not yours. If VPC-only storage is a hard requirement, this is a disqualifying constraint, full stop, and no amount of feature comparison changes that.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="When self-hosting is required" title="Langfuse is the only viable answer to a data residency requirement." body="If your regulatory posture requires that LLM call data never leaves your VPC, Uncoil's managed model doesn't clear the bar — this isn't a trade-off to weigh, it's a disqualifying constraint. Langfuse's self-hosted deployment solves it completely. You give up managed convenience and pay in engineering time, but you get a real, auditable yes to the compliance question instead of a workaround." tone="dark" /%}

> **A middle path exists for some teams.** Langfuse's commercial cloud offering can work for regulated organizations that need managed infrastructure but not physical data residency in their own VPC, provided Langfuse signs a Data Processing Agreement that satisfies your legal team. This isn't the same guarantee as self-hosting — verify the actual compliance posture, subprocessor list, and DPA terms with your own counsel before treating it as equivalent.

### Scenario 3: Growing from 100k to 1M calls a month

The first two scenarios assume a fixed size. The third is about what happens as volume grows tenfold over a year, because the two pricing models scale differently, and this is where Langfuse's cost story genuinely improves relative to Uncoil.

**Langfuse self-hosted** costs are dominated by engineering operations time, not raw infrastructure. Going from 100k to 1M calls a month mostly means bigger Postgres instances and more ingestion workers — infrastructure cost might rise from roughly $1,150 to $2,800 a month, but the 0.5-1.5 FTE of operations overhead does not scale linearly with call volume; the same team that runs the platform at 100k calls a month can often run it at 1M with modest additional tuning. The marginal cost of another 900,000 calls a month is closer to a hardware upgrade than a new hire.

**A SaaS platform priced per event** — whether that's Langfuse Cloud or Uncoil — scales more linearly with volume, because the vendor's own infrastructure and margin are baked into the per-event price. At 1M calls a month, a platform charging roughly $1,000 for 100k events doesn't simply charge $10,000; most vendors offer volume pricing, but the relationship is still closer to linear than the self-hosted infrastructure curve.

This is the honest asymmetry: at moderate scale, Uncoil's all-in-one model wins clearly on total cost because the FTE savings dwarf the platform fee difference. At very high, sustained volume with a team that already has infrastructure expertise, the economics start to favor self-hosting again, because you're amortizing fixed operational overhead across a much larger number of events. If your projected growth curve points toward tens of millions of calls a month within eighteen months, model both curves explicitly rather than assuming today's math holds.

![Bar chart comparing steady-state annual total cost of ownership: Langfuse self-hosted at $129,600, Langfuse Cloud at $51,000, and Uncoil at $30,600, for a 100k-calls-per-month team](/media/langfuse-vs-uncoil-tco-chart.svg)

## Deep comparison: feature by feature

### Tracing and instrumentation

**Langfuse** has mature SDKs across Python, Node.js, and Rust, with an active open-source community contributing integrations. Instrumentation works either through a decorator (`@langfuse()`) or a manual client API for spans, and deep integrations exist for LangChain, LlamaIndex, and the Vercel AI SDK. Span metadata covers model name, token counts, custom tags, user IDs, and session IDs. These SDKs are battle-tested — Langfuse has been running in production at hundreds of companies for years, and it shows in the API's stability.

**Uncoil** offers equivalent SDK coverage across Python, Node.js, and TypeScript, with a TypeScript-first design. Instrumentation is similarly decorator-based (`@uncoil()`) or imperative, and integrations cover LangChain, LlamaIndex, and native OpenAI, Anthropic, and Mistral instrumentation. The meaningful difference is that session tracking is a first-class primitive, not something layered onto span metadata after the fact.

**Where they land:** essentially tied. Both give you a complete, queryable record of every LLM call. Langfuse's longer history shows in the breadth of community integrations; Uncoil's narrower focus on agent workflows shows in how naturally multi-step, multi-turn interactions map onto its primitives. If your workload is a single-shot classifier calling one model, this dimension barely matters. If it's a multi-agent system with retries, tool calls, and conversational memory, the difference becomes more visible in scenario two below.

### Evaluation framework

**Langfuse's** evaluation story starts with manual review: mark a trace good or bad from the dashboard, attach a comment, move on. Beyond that, everything is API-driven — you write scripts that pull traces, apply your own scoring logic (an LLM-as-judge call, a regex, a semantic similarity check), and store the results, typically back in Langfuse as scores or in your own database. There is no built-in test-case management; your evaluation criteria live in a spreadsheet, a markdown file, or a custom test runner you maintain outside the platform. Comparing two prompt versions means writing a comparison script or reading traces side by side manually — there's no built-in experiment abstraction.

**Uncoil** ships a native test-case library: define expected inputs and outputs in the dashboard, tag test cases by version, and re-run them against any batch of traces with one click. Evaluators include exact match, substring match, and LLM-as-judge semantic similarity, all built in — no external evaluation service required for the common cases. An experiment framework lets you compare prompt versions, model choices, or routing strategies with statistical significance testing rather than eyeballing a handful of examples. Regression alerts fire automatically when a metric crosses a threshold you set.

{% highlight %}Evaluation is where the gap between these two products is most decisive. Langfuse's evaluation is a capability you build using their trace API as a foundation. Uncoil's evaluation is a product you use directly. Neither is inherently wrong, but they imply very different engineering investments before you get your first quality gate.{% /highlight %}

If your evaluation logic is genuinely specialized — a proprietary scoring model, a domain-specific rubric that doesn't map to "exact match" or "LLM judge" — Langfuse's open API is an asset, not a gap; you were going to build custom tooling regardless of platform. If your evaluation needs are the common ones (does the output match expected behavior, did quality regress, which prompt version performs better), building that from scratch in Langfuse is largely reinventing what Uncoil already ships. The [practical guide to agent evaluation](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) covers how to design test cases and interpret results in more depth, independent of which platform runs them.

### Cost tracking and analysis

**Langfuse** records token counts at the span level because the model API returns them — that part is free. What it does not do is know what a token costs. Turning "1,200 input tokens, 340 output tokens" into a dollar figure means integrating provider pricing yourself, keeping that pricing current as vendors change their rates, and building the aggregation (cost by model, cost by session, cost trending over time) in your own data warehouse or scripts. You have the raw data; the insight is a project.

**Uncoil** fetches live pricing from OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, and other providers and attaches an exact dollar cost to every span automatically. Each call shows cost, input tokens, output tokens, and model, with no configuration required. Cost trends by session, by agent, and over time are dashboard views, not queries you write. Threshold alerts ("notify us if cost per session exceeds $0.50") and routing recommendations based on cost-versus-quality trade-offs come standard.

**Where they land:** Uncoil, clearly, for teams that need cost visibility without dedicated data engineering. This is a case where the underlying data (tokens) is identical between platforms — the difference is entirely in how much work sits between "we have the data" and "someone on the product team can look at a number and make a decision."

### Session and context tracking

**Langfuse** treats sessions as metadata: you set a session ID on every span yourself, and Langfuse will group by it, but the platform doesn't understand a session as a first-class object with its own lifecycle. Multi-turn context — what happened three turns ago, how a conversation's cost accumulates — is retrievable, but only if your instrumentation was disciplined enough to carry the session ID through every span consistently.

**Uncoil** treats a multi-turn agent interaction as one session unit natively. State, prior turns, and session-level decisions are captured without extra instrumentation discipline, and cost and quality metrics roll up automatically at the session level rather than requiring you to aggregate individual spans after the fact.

**Where they land:** Uncoil, for any product where a single user interaction spans more than one LLM call. If your workload is genuinely single-shot — one call in, one call out, no memory — this dimension is close to irrelevant and you shouldn't weight it heavily in a decision.

### Hosted infrastructure and operations

**Langfuse** offers a real choice here: self-host the entire stack for full control, or use Langfuse Cloud for less operational overhead in exchange for data living in their infrastructure rather than yours. Either way, you or Langfuse decide scaling strategy, and the open-source core means you can always move from cloud to self-hosted later if requirements change.

**Uncoil** is SaaS-only. There is no self-hosted option, which means no infrastructure to run, but also no path to VPC-only data residency, ever. This is a permanent architectural boundary, not a roadmap item.

**Where they land:** entirely dependent on your constraint. If self-hosting is required, Langfuse is the only option on this list. If operational simplicity is the goal, Uncoil wins outright. If you need managed convenience but some contractual data protection, Langfuse Cloud with a DPA is worth evaluating on its own merits.

## Cost analysis: the worked math

Assume a team shipping a production agent system, 100,000 LLM calls a month, roughly twelve engineers, and a three-month runway to production. These figures are representative estimates for reasoning through the decision, not quoted pricing from either vendor — validate against your own infrastructure costs and fully-loaded engineering rates.

### Langfuse, self-hosted

| Category | Detail | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Infrastructure | Postgres primary + replica, Redis, Kubernetes ingestion workers | $1,150/month |
| One-time setup | 3 weeks, two engineers, provisioning and hardening | $16,500 |
| Ongoing operations | 0.6 FTE: on-call, scaling, patching, Postgres maintenance | $9,000/month |
| Evaluation tooling | Build-out plus ongoing maintenance of the evaluation runner | $650/month |
| **Year-one total** | | **$146,100** |
| **Steady-state annual (year two+)** | | **$129,600** |
| Time to production evaluation gate | | ~9 weeks |

### Langfuse Cloud (managed)

| Category | Detail | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | Managed hosting and ingestion, ~100k events/month | $650/month |
| One-time setup | Integration and team onboarding, about 1 week | $2,000 |
| Ongoing operations | ~0.15 FTE: monitoring, vendor support | $2,700/month |
| Evaluation tooling | External LLM-judge service plus glue-code maintenance | $900/month |
| **Year-one total** | | **$53,000** |
| **Steady-state annual (year two+)** | | **$51,000** |
| Time to production evaluation gate | | ~6 weeks |

### Uncoil (integrated SaaS)

| Category | Detail | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | Tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, sessions, experiments, ~100k events/month | $1,050/month |
| One-time setup | Account creation, SDK integration, first test case (2 days) | $500 |
| Ongoing operations | ~0.1 FTE, mostly product-facing, no infrastructure | $1,500/month |
| Evaluation tooling | Included in platform | $0 |
| **Year-one total** | | **$31,100** |
| **Steady-state annual (year two+)** | | **$30,600** |
| Time to production evaluation gate | | ~2-3 days |

{% highlight %}The self-hosted model trades a modest platform fee for engineering time, and engineering time is the expensive line item. Uncoil's steady-state annual cost runs roughly 4.2x cheaper than Langfuse self-hosted at this scale, and it reaches a production evaluation gate five to six weeks faster because evaluation isn't a separate build phase.{% /highlight %}

> **For teams under five engineers:** self-hosted Langfuse is rarely worth the operational tax at this scale — the FTE overhead alone can exceed your entire product engineering budget for a quarter. Langfuse Cloud or Uncoil are the realistic options.
>
> **For teams over twenty engineers with existing infrastructure practice:** self-hosted Langfuse becomes more viable, since the 0.5-1.5 FTE is a smaller fraction of total headcount and may already have a home on an existing platform team.
>
> **For regulated teams:** the self-hosted operational cost is a fixed requirement, not a choice weighed against convenience. Compliance determines the answer before cost does.

## Honest trade-offs: when Langfuse is genuinely the better choice

It would be easy to write this section as a formality before circling back to "but Uncoil wins anyway." That would be dishonest. There are real, specific situations where Langfuse is the correct decision for reasons that have nothing to do with budget, and naming them clearly is the point of this comparison.

**1. Data residency is a hard compliance requirement.** If your regulatory posture mandates that LLM call data never leaves your VPC — common in financial services, healthcare, and government-adjacent work — Uncoil's SaaS-only model does not clear that bar, and no feature advantage changes that. Langfuse self-hosted is the only product on this list that solves the problem completely. Budget 0.5-1.5 FTE for ongoing operations and treat it as a compliance cost, not a discretionary one.

**2. Your team already runs serious infrastructure and treats it as a capability, not a burden.** If you operate Kubernetes at scale, manage Postgres in production, and your security team wants to patch zero-days the hour they're disclosed rather than wait on a vendor's release cycle, self-hosting Langfuse plays to genuine organizational strength. The 2-3 week setup and 0.5-1 FTE ongoing cost is a fair price for that level of control, and it's a cost this kind of team is already structured to absorb.

**3. Your evaluation needs are genuinely specialized.** If you're running proprietary ML scoring models, integrating with an internal evaluation platform, or building evaluation logic that doesn't map cleanly onto "exact match," "semantic similarity," or "LLM judge," Langfuse's open trace API is a better foundation than trying to bend Uncoil's test-case model to fit. You own the glue code either way; Langfuse just gives you a cleaner starting point for writing it.

**4. You already have significant Langfuse instrumentation in production.** Migration cost is real. Rewriting spans, redirecting SDKs, and rebuilding evaluation logic elsewhere is genuine engineering effort — usually two to four weeks for a large codebase. If you're not planning to run this system for another twelve-plus months, the migration may simply not pay for itself, regardless of which platform is theoretically better suited to your next project.

### Where Uncoil is the better choice

**1. Production confidence is the binding constraint and the clock is running.** If you have three months and evaluation infrastructure cannot be a separate phase after tracing ships, Uncoil's integrated model reaches a working quality gate in days instead of weeks. This is the single largest practical difference between the two products.

**2. Cost transparency is a business requirement, not just an engineering nice-to-have.** If product and finance leadership need per-request cost, trending, and model-selection recommendations without routing every question through a data engineer, Uncoil's automatic pricing removes that dependency entirely.

**3. Your team is small enough that operational overhead is a real tax.** Under roughly eight engineers, 0.5-1.5 FTE dedicated to infrastructure operations is a meaningful fraction of your entire capacity. SaaS simplicity is worth the trade-off of not owning the infrastructure.

**4. Multi-turn agent interactions are central to the product.** If users interact with your agent over several turns and you care about session-level cost and quality more than individual call metrics, Uncoil's native session model is simpler than building the equivalent correlation logic in Langfuse yourself.

### A hybrid worth naming, and one worth avoiding

Some larger teams run Langfuse for tracing and route evaluation to Uncoil, Braintrust, or an internal tool. This gets you best-of-breed components at the cost of maintaining two integrations, manual cost reconciliation between systems, and no native session management spanning both. It's a defensible choice for large teams with genuinely specialized evaluation needs who can absorb the operational overhead of running two platforms.

What's not defensible is running both "just in case," indefinitely, without a decision date. That doubles your integration surface and your monthly cost without doubling your insight. If you're piloting both to compare evaluation quality, put a decision date on the calendar before you start.

![Decision-tree diagram: does production data need to stay in your VPC, branching to Langfuse self-hosted if yes, or to a second question about evaluation velocity that branches to Uncoil or Langfuse Cloud, with a migration-asymmetry panel below showing Langfuse-to-Uncoil as low-risk and reversible within 24 hours versus Uncoil-to-Langfuse as a 4-6 week rebuild](/media/langfuse-vs-uncoil-decision-path.svg)

## Decision checklist

Use this table to score your own situation. It's a reasoning tool, not a formula — read the "suggests" column even where your answer surprises you.

| Question | If yes, weight toward | Why it matters |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Must production data stay inside your VPC? | Langfuse (heavily) | This alone can be disqualifying for Uncoil, regardless of every other factor |
| Is evaluation velocity your primary blocker to shipping? | Uncoil | Langfuse requires you to build the evaluation layer before you can use it |
| Do you have 1.5+ FTE available for platform operations? | Langfuse | Self-hosting only pays off when ops capacity genuinely exists, not just headcount on paper |
| Are you already running Langfuse in production today? | Langfuse | Migration cost is real; don't discount it just because a competitor looks better on paper |
| Do you need LLM-as-judge evaluation without external tooling? | Uncoil | This is built in on Uncoil and DIY on Langfuse |
| Is cost transparency a Q1 business requirement from leadership? | Uncoil | Automatic per-call pricing removes a dependency on data engineering |
| Do you want to audit or modify the platform's source code? | Langfuse | Open-source gives you this option; Uncoil does not |
| Is your workload dominated by multi-turn, stateful agent sessions? | Uncoil | Native session tracking beats manually correlated span metadata |

If most of your answers cluster toward Langfuse, self-hosting is worth serious evaluation, especially if data residency is one of them. If most cluster toward Uncoil, the operational simplicity and evaluation speed are likely to outweigh what you give up in architectural control. If the answers split roughly evenly, revisit which single constraint your organization actually cannot compromise on — usually there's one that dominates the rest once you name it explicitly.

## Migration paths and reversibility

**From Langfuse to Uncoil is comparatively low-risk.** Add the Uncoil SDK alongside your existing Langfuse instrumentation and send identical spans to both platforms for two to four weeks. Build equivalent test cases in Uncoil and compare evaluation results against what you're already getting from Langfuse, and verify that Uncoil's automatic cost calculations line up with your expectations. If the comparison looks good, redirect instrumentation fully to Uncoil and keep Langfuse running for another two weeks as a safety net before shutting it down. You can roll back to Langfuse within about 24 hours at any point in this process, which makes it a genuinely low-risk way to evaluate the switch.

**From Uncoil to Langfuse is more expensive.** Export traces and evaluation results through Uncoil's API, then prepare a self-hosted Langfuse environment to receive them. Expect two to four weeks of manual work to replay traces and recreate your test cases and evaluation logic — there's no automated migration tool for this direction. Run both systems in parallel for another two weeks to validate trace fidelity and evaluation results before cutting over fully and decommissioning Uncoil. Total risk is moderate to high, mostly because rebuilding evaluation logic by hand takes real time. If there's a real chance you'll need self-hosting later, it's worth deciding that early rather than migrating into it after the fact.

> **Trade-off:** Langfuse maximizes deployment control through self-hosting; Uncoil minimizes the operational work required to connect traces, evaluation, and cost decisions.

## Action checklist for decision-makers

- [ ] **Compliance check.** Document your actual data residency requirements in writing. If VPC-only storage is required, Langfuse self-hosted is your only viable path — settle this first, before comparing anything else.
- [ ] **Scope check.** Estimate monthly LLM call volume, team size, and your real time-to-production deadline, not an optimistic one.
- [ ] **Cost check.** Calculate a real TCO for each option: platform fees plus engineering time, not just the sticker price of a subscription or an open-source license.
- [ ] **Evaluation check.** Write down what your quality gates actually need to test, then verify whether the platform's evaluation model supports them natively or requires custom tooling.
- [ ] **Operations check.** Honestly assess your team's appetite and available capacity for infrastructure operations versus wanting operational simplicity.
- [ ] **Prototype.** If you're genuinely undecided, run a one-to-two-week parallel pilot sending the same spans to both platforms — the SDKs are compatible enough to instrument once.
- [ ] **Reversibility.** Plan your migration path and set a concrete decision date before you start, so a pilot doesn't quietly become permanent dual-running.
- [ ] **Review.** Set a 30-day review date after committing, to check trace fidelity, cost accuracy, and whether the evaluation framework is actually catching what matters.

If speed and integrated evaluation are the priority, start with the [complete guide to agent observability](/blog/ai-agent-observability-complete-guide) to understand what a full observability stack needs to capture, then evaluate Uncoil against that bar. If self-hosting and architectural control are the priority, Langfuse's own deployment documentation and community forums are the right next stop for the operational detail this article doesn't attempt to replace.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Is Langfuse actually expensive to self-host?" answer="Not in licensing terms — the software itself is free and open-source. But the fully loaded cost is higher than teams often expect going in. At roughly 100k calls a month, budget infrastructure around $1,150/month (Postgres, Redis, Kubernetes ingestion workers), a one-time setup cost around $16,500 for two to three weeks of engineering time, and ongoing operations around 0.6 FTE, which typically runs $9,000/month or more depending on your region and seniority mix. That puts steady-state annual cost around $129,600 in this scenario, roughly 4.2x what an integrated SaaS platform costs at the same scale. It's often still cheaper than commercial alternatives at very large scale with a mature infrastructure team, but for teams under about eight engineers, the operational overhead usually isn't worth it." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can we run Langfuse and Uncoil side by side while we decide?" answer="Yes, and it's a reasonable way to de-risk the decision. Both platforms can ingest identical span data, so you instrument your code once and send traces to both simultaneously for two to four weeks. Build equivalent test cases in each and compare evaluation results directly, and check whether Uncoil's automatic cost calculations match what you'd compute manually from Langfuse's token data. The marginal cost of running both in parallel briefly is low since you're not duplicating engineering effort, just data destinations. The one thing to avoid is running both indefinitely without a decision date — set one before you start the pilot, or the comparison quietly turns into permanent dual maintenance." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does Langfuse track cost the way Uncoil does?" answer="Not out of the box. Langfuse records token counts at the span level because the model provider's API returns them for free, but it has no concept of what a token costs in dollars. To get an Uncoil-style cost breakdown, you need to integrate provider pricing yourself, keep it current as vendors adjust rates, and build the aggregation — cost by model, by session, trending over time — in your own data warehouse or scripts. This is entirely doable, and plenty of teams do it, but it's ongoing engineering work rather than a feature you turn on. Uncoil fetches live pricing from OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, and other providers and attaches an exact cost to every call automatically, with no pricing table to maintain on your end." /%}

{% faq-item question="How much harder is evaluation on Langfuse compared to Uncoil?" answer="Meaningfully harder if you want the common case (test cases, batch runs, LLM-as-judge, regression alerts) rather than something highly custom. On Langfuse, evaluation starts as manual review through the dashboard — marking traces good or bad — and anything automated means writing your own runner against the Langfuse API: pulling traces, applying your scoring logic, storing results, and building your own regression detection. There's no built-in test-case versioning or experiment comparison. On Uncoil, all of that is native: define test cases in the dashboard, run batch evaluations with a click, get automated regression alerts, and compare prompt or model versions with built-in statistical testing. The trade-off is real: Langfuse gives you full control if your evaluation logic is genuinely non-standard; Uncoil gives you speed if it isn't." /%}

{% faq-item question="When should we genuinely pick Langfuse over Uncoil, not just as a fallback?" answer="Four situations make Langfuse the right call on the merits, not as a compromise: data residency is a hard compliance requirement and production data cannot leave your VPC (Langfuse self-hosted is the only option here); your team already runs serious infrastructure and treats operational ownership as a strength rather than overhead; your evaluation needs are genuinely specialized in a way that doesn't map to standard test-case patterns, so you're building custom tooling regardless of platform; or you already have significant Langfuse instrumentation in production and the migration cost outweighs the benefit of switching. Outside of those four situations, most teams optimizing for evaluation velocity, cost transparency, and time to production will move faster on an integrated platform. The fastest way to check which camp you're in: can you self-host today, and do you need built-in evaluation this quarter? Those two answers usually settle it." /%}
